"use strict";(self.webpackChunksite=self.webpackChunksite||[]).push([["607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"hello-bolt","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/hello-bolt","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-10-24-hello-bolt.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-24-hello-bolt.mdx","title":"The Resurrection of Bolt","description":"For a long time, I\'ve been an avid opposer of generative AI. My queries were","date":"2026-10-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Announcements","permalink":"/blog/tags/announcements","description":"Important notifications concerning the Bolt programming language"},{"inline":false,"label":"Reflections","permalink":"/blog/tags/reflections","description":"Non-technical discussions circling around development and open-source"}],"readingTime":3.12,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Sam Vervaeck","title":"Open-source developer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/vervaeck-sam","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/samvv"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/vervaeck-sam/","github":"https://github.com/samvv"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/samvv.png","key":"samvv"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"hello-bolt","title":"The Resurrection of Bolt","authors":["samvv"],"tags":["announcement","reflection"]},"unlisted":false},"content":"For a long time, I\'ve been an avid opposer of generative AI. My queries were\\nlimited to some targeted questions about certain libraries and how to manage\\nKubernetes. I mainly used Gemini, and didn\'t put much thought in that decision.\\nThe craze about AI coding agents went largely past me.\\n\\nThat is until now.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## A Sisyphean Task\\n\\nWriting a compiler is an enourmous undertaking. Especially with elaborate\\nsyntax such as the one Bolt uses, there are hundreds, if not thousands of cases\\nto cover in the parser and type checker alone. Elaborating to a core language\\nduring type checking may make the backend more feasible, but since Bolt\\nrequires a garbage collector it still is a tremendous amount of work on its own.\\n\\nAs a consequence, the Bolt parser and a small but essential part of the type\\nchecker was the furthest I ever was able to go. I made prototypes in different\\nlanguages, eventually settling for Rust. Things like tuples, polymorphic\\nfunctions and extensible records worked beautifully. However, the more features\\nI added, the bigger the backlog grew, and eventually I gave up.\\n\\nFor example: I noticed way too late that using the CST (backed by Rowan)\\ndirectly in the type checker is far from ideal. rust-analyzer takes a whole\\ndifferent approach by collecting properties in different structures in its\\n`hir` and `hir-def` crates. This opens the door for the type checker to be\\nsimpler, more effient, and allows it to make use of the Salsa database. I\\nsimply didn\'t have the remaining energy to perform this refactor.\\n\\n## AI To The Rescue\\n\\nBecause I was completely burried under the amount of work to do, I made a\\ndesparate move. A week ago, I subscribed to Claude code. Not expecting much\\nfrom it, I instructed it to fork the Rust tree-sitter grammar and clean it up\\nso that it might become a base for a Bolt grammar. In a later stage I would\\nthen use this grammar to correctly implement the compiler\'s parser.\\n\\nTo my great surprise the AI did remarkably well. It found all the occurrences\\nof productions that should be removed and cleanly reduced the grammar to its\\nessence. When I asked it to add some new rules based on some examples alone, it\\ndid not fail to disappoint. In under half an hour I had my first prototype of a\\nBolt tree-sitter grammar.\\n\\nThe surprises didn\'t stop there. I asked Claude simply to synchronize the\\ncompiler\'s parser with the grammar. This would have taken me several days, if\\nnot weeks to complete. The AI not only succeeded in making the parser comply to\\nthe tree-sitter grammar, but also wrote additional tests and even imported the\\ntest corpus of the tree-sitter grammar so that it could run it with the\\ncompiler\'s parser.\\n\\nI was sold.\\n\\nA week later, and Claude has written a type checker and name resolution system\\nthat deeply integrates with Salsa. The compiler now has basic support for\\ntypeclasses, something that I only managed to accomplish once on a prototype\\nyears ago. I now have a playground, and a testing infrastructure that is both\\nreadable and integrates neatly with `cargo test`. I could never have imagined\\nthat it would go this fast.\\n\\nLooking to the future, I see myself continue using Claude for Bolt. I had\\nthe idea that the tests should be thoroughly reviewed by a human, and that any\\nimplementation of the Bolt compiler should pass all tests. That way one can be\\nsure the compiler behaves correctly, no matter how bad or good the code is, or\\nwhether it is generated by AI or not.\\n\\nIn the end, I want to ship software, and AI makes that possible."}]}}')}}]);